      "No ductwork, plumbing or electrician needed",
      "Rated about 11.44 gallons per day for up to 3,600 sq ft",
      "Evaporative, so no white dust",
      "Built-in humidistat, listed range 25 to 65 percent"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Must be filled by hand, often daily at full output",
      "Coverage drops across multiple floors",
      "Wick filters are an ongoing cost",
      "Two-year warranty is short next to ducted units"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The AIRCARE MA1201 is the whole house humidifier for homes without forced-air ducts or for renters, as long as you accept refilling it by hand."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Whole House Humidifiers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-whole-house-humidifier/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best whole house humidifier?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homes with a gas or oil furnace, the Aprilaire 600 is the best whole house humidifier. Aprilaire rates it at 17 gallons per day and sizes it for up to 4,000 square feet in a tight home or 2,000 in an average one. Its automatic control adjusts humidity to the outdoor temperature, which prevents window condensation."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a bypass or fan-powered humidifier better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They perform similarly; the difference is installation. A bypass unit needs room for a 6-inch duct between plenums and has no fan to fail. A fan-powered unit like the Aprilaire 700 needs 120-volt power but no bypass duct and offers slightly more output. Choose based on your ductwork and what your installer recommends."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a steam humidifier with a heat pump?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In most cases, yes. Evaporative units rely on warm supply air to evaporate water, and heat pumps deliver cooler air than gas furnaces. A steam humidifier makes its own heat, so it works regardless of supply temperature. Expect a dedicated circuit, higher electricity use and periodic canister replacement."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should I replace the water panel?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Aprilaire sells its #35 panel as a once-per-heating-season replacement, and retailers recommend more often on hard water. Sylvane suggests twice per season for the Honeywell HC26-series pads. Replace it at the start of every heating season at minimum, and inspect it midwinter if your water is hard."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I install a whole house humidifier myself?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A capable DIYer can, but it involves cutting the plenum, tapping a water line, running a drain and low-voltage wiring. Aprilaire recommends professional installation, and some warranties depend on it. Steam units also need a dedicated electrical circuit, which is a job for a licensed electrician in most areas."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What humidity should a whole house humidifier be set to?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Generally 30 to 40 percent in moderate cold, lower as it gets colder outside, sometimes into the 20s on the coldest nights in cold climates. The limit is window condensation. An automatic control with an outdoor sensor handles this for you; with a manual humidistat, turn it down before cold snaps."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a whole house humidifier cause mold?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not if it is sized and controlled properly. Mold problems come from humidity set too high for the outdoor temperature, which causes condensation on cold windows and walls, or from a neglected unit with standing water. Use an outdoor-sensor control, check rooms with a hygrometer, and service the unit every season."
          }
        }
