    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          For most homes with a gas furnace, the Aprilaire 600 is the whole house humidifier to buy. Heat pump owners need steam, and homes without ducts need a console. The rest of this guide is about getting the size and the controls right, which matters more than the brand.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked These Humidifiers
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not install six furnace humidifiers in a test house and run them for a winter, and we will not pretend otherwise. This guide is built from manufacturer specification sheets, retailer listings that repeat the manufacturer's ratings, installation manuals and the way these units are actually specified by HVAC contractors. Every capacity, coverage and electrical figure on this page is attributed to its source, and where sources disagree we say so.
          </p>

          <p>
            That approach suits this category better than you might think. Whole house humidifiers are mature, mechanically simple products. A bypass unit is a water valve, a distribution tray, an evaporator pad and a duct. What separates a good result from a bad one is mostly sizing, the type of heating system, the control strategy and the quality of the installation. Those are the things this guide spends its time on.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighted four things. First, the right type for the heating system, because a bypass humidifier on a heat pump is a waste of money. Second, honest capacity for real home sizes, using the manufacturer's tightness tiers rather than the biggest number on the box. Third, controls, because an automatic humidistat with an outdoor sensor prevents most of the problems people blame on humidifiers. Fourth, the cost and availability of consumables, since a water panel or steam canister you cannot find locally is a problem every winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are still deciding whether you want a whole house system at all or a few room units, start with our <a href="/best-humidifier/">best humidifier guide</a>, which covers portable models, and our <a href="/whole-house-humidifier/">whole house humidifier explainer</a>, which covers how these systems work and what they cost to install. This page assumes you have decided on whole house and want to know which unit to put on your furnace.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Bypass vs Fan-Powered vs Steam
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every furnace-mounted humidifier falls into one of three designs, and the choice is mostly made for you by your heating system and home size. Pick the type first and the model second.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bypass</h3>

          <p>
            A bypass humidifier mounts on the supply or return plenum and connects to the other side with a short, usually 6-inch, duct. When the furnace blower runs, pressure difference pushes some warm air through the bypass, across a water-soaked panel, and back into the system carrying moisture. There is no fan and no heater, so it is cheap, quiet and reliable. The Aprilaire 600, Honeywell HE280 and GeneralAire 1042LH are all bypass units. The limitation is that it only works while the blower runs and works best with hot supply air from a gas or oil furnace.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fan-powered</h3>

          <p>
            A fan-powered unit is the same evaporative idea with its own fan. It pulls air from the plenum through the panel without needing a bypass duct, which helps where there is no room to run one, and it typically delivers a little more output from a similar footprint. The Aprilaire 700 is the standard example. You need 120-volt power at the furnace, and the fan is a part that can eventually wear out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Steam</h3>

          <p>
            A steam humidifier boils water electrically and injects steam into the duct. Because it does not rely on warm air to evaporate water, it is the type to choose with a heat pump, a high-efficiency furnace that runs long cycles at lower supply temperatures, or a large house that a bypass unit cannot keep up with. It also controls humidity more precisely. The costs are a dedicated electrical circuit, much higher electricity use and a canister that needs periodic replacement. The Aprilaire 800 is our steam pick.
          </p>

